Secure Boot provides a way to prevent unnoticed tampering with the boot chain.
Using Secure Boot requires signing different parts of the boot chain. At boot,
these signatures need to be verified before the respective part is executed.

Additionally, on-device firmware, known as Option ROM, can also be
factory-signed and make use of Secure Boot for verification. For these
signatures to be verified, the matching public key must be present in the EFI
variables. For plug and play devices such as GPUs, manufacturers have no way of
enrolling their own keys into the user's firmware. They instead sign their
firmware with Microsoft's certificates since these are assumed to be enrolled on
all consumer systems. These devices will not work if Microsoft's keys are not
enrolled in the EFI variables. To make sure factory-signed devices work without
issue, we will also enroll Microsoft's secure boot keys during this step. As
there are no downsides to this, enrolling these keys is non-optional.

On OEM hardware such as laptops, the firmware may come with additional keys
enrolled, on which some of the devices may depend. If these devices are critical
to the boot process and the corresponding keys are missing, you may end up in a
situation where the computer does not boot and there is no way to enter the
BIOS, effectively bricking your device. For this reason, Secure Boot can be
dangerous if set up improperly and on devices with factory-signed firmware. For
desktop, non-OEM computers, the actual danger is miniscule but for laptops, it
is advised to at least check the Arch Wiki for an entry for your device. If your
device has any issues with Secure Boot, they are usually listed there.

This script takes the necessary precautions to bypass any potentially
troublesome effects of setting up Secure Boot. However, you yourself are
responsible for making sure you know what you are doing when running this
script. I take no responsibility for any damage your system may sustain.

